<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="IRScan.app" />

  <!--
    requireAdministrator, and the reasoning matters because the alternative is
    tempting.

    This tool exists to answer "is this machine compromised?", and an answer is only
    as good as the evidence behind it. Without elevation the Security event log is
    unreadable (Win32 error 5), Prefetch is unavailable, and protected processes
    refuse to name their own image - three blind spots in exactly the places a hidden
    agent leaves traces. An unelevated scan is not a smaller scan, it is a scan whose
    clean result means less, and this tool's whole design is built on refusing that
    ambiguity.

    Declaring the level in the manifest rather than self-elevating through
    ShellExecute "runas" changes what Windows shows the operator: the shield appears
    on the icon and the prompt is raised by the OS before the process starts, so there
    is no window in which the tool runs with less access than it needs. The
    self-elevation path in `win::elevate` is kept because it remains the mechanism for
    re-running from inside a session, and it is harmless here: `win::is_elevated`
    reads the real token, so a process started under this manifest reports elevated
    and never relaunches itself.

    `uiAccess` stays false. It would let the tool send input to higher-integrity
    windows, which a read-only triage tool must not be able to do.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="requireAdministrator" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 / 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <!--
    The tool draws its own monochrome interface and enables virtual-terminal
    processing itself, so it needs no manifest for that. It DOES need to be told it is
    DPI-aware, or Windows bitmap-stretches the console on a scaled display and the
    right-aligned column of the verdict block wraps.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
